<!--
  This is an EXAMPLE. Copy it to `SYSTEM.md` in this project root to take it into use;
  nothing reads this file at launch. `SYSTEM.md` is the main agent's own voice, staged
  into `~/.kimi-code/SYSTEM.md` and delivered to the main agent alone - a subagent gets
  the contract in `CONTEXT.md` and never sees this document.

  Three states, and they mean different things:

    no SYSTEM.md    Kimi Code's built-in system prompt is used, untouched. This is the
                    default, and it is not the same as the file being empty.
    SYSTEM.md       Whatever you write here, in full. It replaces the built-in prompt
                    unless you ask for it below.
    empty SYSTEM.md An empty prompt. The startup panel's add-ons are appended after it,
                    so an empty file plus add-ons is a session whose entire system prompt
                    is those add-ons; an empty file with every add-on switched off is a
                    single period, which is as close to silence as Kimi Code permits.

  Template values. Kimi Code substitutes these when it renders the prompt, so they are
  available here whatever else you do:

    ${base_prompt}        Kimi Code's own built-in system prompt, in full
    ${agents_md}          the staged contract - `CONTEXT.md` plus its generated sections
    ${cwd} ${os} ${shell} where the session is running
    ${cwd_listing}        a directory listing of the workspace
    ${product_name} ${reply_style_guide} ${role_additional}
    ${skills} ${skills_section} ${plugin_sections}
    ${additional_dirs_info} ${additional_dirs_section}
    ${notify_user_guidance} ${windows_notes}

  `${base_prompt}` may appear at most once; a second copy would duplicate the whole
  built-in prompt. It also already contains the contract, so do not write `${agents_md}`
  beside it - that names the same text twice and ships it twice. Reference `${agents_md}`
  on its own instead, in a prompt that replaces Kimi Code's rather than wrapping it.

  Anything that is not one of the names above is left exactly as written, so a shell
  example like ${HOME} in your own prose is safe - but a misspelling of a real name is
  caught at launch rather than shipped to the model as a literal.

  HTML comments like this one are stripped at staging and never reach the model, so a
  file you maintain can explain itself for free.
-->

${base_prompt}

<!--
  Everything below this line is yours. This wrapper keeps Kimi Code's own prompt and adds
  a single rule to it, which is the common case. To write the whole prompt yourself,
  delete the wrapper line above and start from scratch - including ${agents_md} somewhere
  in it, which is how the contract gets there once the prompt that carried it is gone.
-->

Reply in the language the user writes in.
